From 3dea05af5c008ad36d5edf0f774e2f6f05d11d06 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hiby-r1-linux-kernel-compiling <kernel@hiby-r1.local>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 20:46:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] mm/page_alloc: don't reserve a highatomic pageblock
 that is the whole zone

This board sets CONFIG_FORCE_MAX_ZONEORDER=15 (from Ingenic's own SDK
defconfig), which makes pageblock_nr_pages 16384 pages = 64MB, against a
single Normal zone of ~56MB. "Page block order: 14" and one block for the
entire zone, confirmed on hardware via /proc/pagetypeinfo.

The consequence is that the first successful order>0 ALLOC_HARDER
allocation converts the ENTIRE zone to MIGRATE_HIGHATOMIC. Every
non-atomic allocation then fails while free memory looks abundant, reclaim
reports all_unreclaimable, and the OOM killer runs. Captured on real
hardware during a WiFi download writing to the SD card:

  Normal free:38912kB min:8192kB low:10240kB ... all_unreclaimable? yes
  Normal: 648*4kB (H) 482*8kB (H) 293*16kB (H) ... = 38912kB
  Out of memory: Kill process 1020 (library_standal)
  Kernel panic - not syncing: Out of memory and no killable processes...

38MB free, every block (H), OOM anyway. Raising min_free_kbytes to 8MB does
not help -- it only gives the reserve more to swallow, and the failure gets
worse rather than better.

The existing cap cannot prevent this. max_managed is
managed_pages/100 + pageblock_nr_pages, which here is ~66MB, larger than
the 56MB zone: nr_reserved_highatomic can never reach it, so the check
never fires. It is not an accounting bug -- the limit is structurally
unreachable whenever one pageblock exceeds the zone.

Refuse the reservation when a pageblock is a large fraction of the zone.
The highatomic reserve is an optimisation for high-order atomic
allocations, not a correctness requirement; declining it means they come
from the normal free lists instead.

Guarding here rather than lowering FORCE_MAX_ZONEORDER is deliberate: that
would also shrink the largest contiguous allocation available to this
platform's closed-source DMA-hungry drivers (HGL alone wants 6MB), which is
a far riskier change than declining an optimisation.
---
 mm/page_alloc.c | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+)

diff --git a/mm/page_alloc.c b/mm/page_alloc.c
index 6b5421ae..5e83d458 100644
--- a/mm/page_alloc.c
+++ b/mm/page_alloc.c
@@ -1689,6 +1689,32 @@ static void reserve_highatomic_pageblock(struct page *page, struct zone *zone,
 	int mt;
 	unsigned long max_managed, flags;
 
+	/*
+	 * Refuse entirely when one pageblock is a large fraction of the whole
+	 * zone. This board sets CONFIG_FORCE_MAX_ZONEORDER=15, giving
+	 * pageblock_nr_pages == 16384 (64MB) against a single ~56MB Normal
+	 * zone -- so reserving even one pageblock converts the ENTIRE zone to
+	 * MIGRATE_HIGHATOMIC, after which every non-atomic allocation fails
+	 * while free memory looks abundant. Observed on real hardware: OOM
+	 * (and eventually "Out of memory and no killable processes" panic)
+	 * with 38MB free, every free block tagged (H).
+	 *
+	 * The existing cap below cannot prevent this: max_managed works out to
+	 * managed/100 + pageblock_nr_pages, which here is ~66MB -- larger than
+	 * the 56MB zone, so nr_reserved_highatomic can never reach it and the
+	 * check never fires.
+	 *
+	 * The reserve is an optimisation for high-order atomic allocations,
+	 * not a correctness requirement; declining it simply means those come
+	 * from the normal free lists, which is the behaviour on a kernel that
+	 * never happens to take this path at all. Guarding here rather than
+	 * lowering FORCE_MAX_ZONEORDER deliberately: that would also shrink the
+	 * largest contiguous allocation available to the vendor's DMA-hungry
+	 * closed-source drivers, which is a much riskier change.
+	 */
+	if (pageblock_nr_pages * 4 > zone->managed_pages)
+		return;
+
 	/*
 	 * Limit the number reserved to 1 pageblock or roughly 1% of a zone.
 	 * Check is race-prone but harmless.
-- 
